PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Q. II, III, V

I agree with the colleagues who favor II, III, and V. The clinical guidelines (SAMHSA, ACOG) are explicit that Subutex is preferred in pregnancy or documented naloxone sensitivity precisely to avoid even low-level precipitated withdrawal risk, which directly backs II. III is also on solid ground: when both products are taken sublingually as directed, naloxone bioavailability is negligible, so the therapeutic safety and efficacy profiles are driven by buprenorphine alone and are essentially equivalent in registration trials and post-marketing data. V adds the important route-dependent nuance that the only established safety differential is parenteral misuse deterrence; once we restrict to prescribed oral/sublingual use, the profiles converge. 

R’s restriction to only II and III feels unnecessarily narrow—it discards the well-documented abuse-liability advantage that applies when patients deviate from the prescribed route, which is a real-world safety consideration repeatedly cited in the literature. The minor wording issue some have flagged in V does not change the underlying pharmacology or the evidence that the naloxone component’s protective effect is route-specific. This combination of statements best captures the full evidence base without over- or under-stating what we know.}

