PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Q. II, III, V

I agree with the colleagues who have landed on Q. Statement II is directly supported by longstanding guidelines (SAMHSA, ACOG) that explicitly favor the mono-product in pregnancy or documented naloxone sensitivity precisely to avoid any risk of precipitated withdrawal. Statement III follows from the core pharmacology: when either formulation is taken sublingually as directed, buprenorphine drives the therapeutic effect and adverse-event profile, rendering the two products essentially bioequivalent in routine use. Statement V captures the remaining evidence-based distinction—that the naloxone component’s protective effect is confined to the parenteral route, so comparative safety is route-dependent. 

The objections raised against V (chiefly its wording about “lack of naloxone”) strike me as overly literal; the underlying point about route-specific risk reduction is well-established in the literature and aligns with how the products are labeled and prescribed. Meanwhile I inverts the abuse-deterrent rationale and IV overstates uncertainty where decades of comparative data already exist. This leaves II, III, and V as the statements that best match the evidence.}

