PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to see Q (II, III, V) as the combination that best matches the evidence without discarding a well-documented clinical distinction. II and III are uncontroversial: guidelines (SAMHSA, ACOG, FDA labeling) explicitly favor the mono-product in pregnancy or documented naloxone sensitivity to avoid any risk of precipitated withdrawal, and sublingual PK studies show both formulations are bioequivalent for buprenorphine when taken as directed, producing identical therapeutic safety/efficacy profiles. 

The remaining question is V. Several colleagues correctly note its awkward phrasing (“due to the lack of naloxone”), yet the underlying pharmacology the statement is trying to capture—that any differential safety effect is confined to the parenteral route—is directly supported by the original abuse-liability trials, post-marketing surveillance, and current labeling. When either product is used sublingually, naloxone contributes negligible bioavailability and no added risk; the only established safety advantage appears when the medication is injected. That route-specific distinction is why prescribing information and clinical practice already stratify recommendations by indication and administration method rather than declaring one formulation universally safer. 

If we treat the wording slip as a minor drafting artifact rather than a fatal factual reversal, V survives alongside II and III. Statements I and IV remain unsupported (I reverses the abuse-deterrent logic of naloxone; IV overstates residual uncertainty after >20 years of comparative data). This keeps the supported statements precisely at II, III, and V. Colleagues favoring R, could you clarify whether the wording issue alone is decisive enough to exclude an otherwise pharmacologically accurate observation, or whether a modest rephrasing would bring the two positions into alignment?}

